    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "the fluid looks pretty clean though, no burnt smell or anything. if it's not the fluid, could it be a sensor issue or is it more likely something mechanical like the solenoids or the valve body itself? how would i test for that?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "While contaminated fluid is common, clean fluid suggests you should focus on electrical or internal mechanical issues. Sensor malfunctions can certainly cause erratic readings, but pressure spiking is often a symptom of sticking or worn solenoids or valve body passages. To test, start by checking solenoid electrical signals and wiring integrity, and if those seem fine, a pressure bleed-down test targeting the valve body is the next logical step.",
      "source": "target",
